{
  "green-gold-what-spirulina-actually-is": {
    "picture_html": "<figure style=\"margin:1.6rem 0\">\n<img src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Biotechnology-_the_research_on_the_production_of_biofuels_from_microalgae_%28P-016345-03%29.jpg/960px-Biotechnology-_the_research_on_the_production_of_biofuels_from_microalgae_%28P-016345-03%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" alt=\"Spirulina cultivated in open, shallow ponds, the growing method described in this piece\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;display:block\"/>\n<figcaption class=\"gb-note\" style=\"margin-top:.4rem\">Spirulina is harvested by filtering the cyanobacterium out of shallow, warm ponds like these — the low-input growing method that keeps its land and water footprint far below any row crop. <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biotechnology-_the_research_on_the_production_of_biofuels_from_microalgae_(P-016345-03).jpg\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Culture of spirulina in ponds</a>, Sipa Press / Laurent Chamussy, © European Union, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption>\n</figure>"
  },
  "greenhouse-emissions-reduction-and-cbam": {
    "picture_html": "<figure style=\"margin:1.6rem 0\">\n<img src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Bokaro_Steel_Plant.jpg/960px-Bokaro_Steel_Plant.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" alt=\"Bokaro Steel Plant, one of India's integrated steel plants exposed to EU carbon border tariffs\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;display:block\"/>\n<figcaption class=\"gb-note\" style=\"margin-top:.4rem\">India's integrated steel plants — like SAIL's Bokaro works, pictured here — sit behind the $8.2 billion of EU-bound iron and steel exports now facing CBAM's carbon tariff. <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bokaro_Steel_Plant.jpg\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bokaro Steel Plant</a>, SuLTan0203, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption>\n</figure>"
  },
  "guar-gum-indias-35-billion-export-peak": {
    "picture_html": "<figure style=\"margin:1.6rem 0\">\n<img src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Guar_seeds_%287856427394%29.jpg/960px-Guar_seeds_%287856427394%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" alt=\"Guar seeds from the cluster bean plant, the source of the export commodity this piece tracks\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;display:block\"/>\n<figcaption class=\"gb-note\" style=\"margin-top:.4rem\">Guar gum is extracted from seeds like these, from the drought-tolerant cluster bean (Cyamopsis tetragonoloba) that India grows across Rajasthan, Haryana and Gujarat. <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guar_seeds_(7856427394).jpg\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Guar seeds</a>, Ton Rulkens, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption>\n</figure>"
  },
  "half-of-indias-import-bill-is": {
    "picture_html": "<figure style=\"margin:1.6rem 0\">\n<img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/32/Jamnagar_Refinery.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled\" alt=\"Jamnagar Refinery in Gujarat, which processes much of the crude oil behind India's mineral-fuels import bill\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;display:block\"/>\n<figcaption class=\"gb-note\" style=\"margin-top:.4rem\">Mineral fuels — crude, products, coal and LNG processed at refineries like Jamnagar — make up the single largest slice of the $372 billion commodity import bill this piece traces. <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jamnagar_Refinery.jpg\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Jamnagar Refinery</a>, Reliance Industries, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption>\n</figure>"
  },
  "how-india-actually-plans-to-hit-net": {
    "picture_html": "<figure style=\"margin:1.6rem 0\">\n<img src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Suratgarh_Super_Thermal_Power_Plant.JPG/960px-Suratgarh_Super_Thermal_Power_Plant.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" alt=\"Suratgarh coal-fired thermal power plant in Rajasthan, part of the rising coal curve inside India's Net Zero pathway\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;display:block\"/>\n<figcaption class=\"gb-note\" style=\"margin-top:.4rem\">Coal-fired capacity like Suratgarh's is exactly what keeps rising for another two decades even inside NITI Aayog's Net Zero 2070 scenarios — the rising coal curve this piece maps against the CCUS bet meant to offset it. <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suratgarh_Super_Thermal_Power_Plant.JPG\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Suratgarh Super Thermal Power Plant</a>, Ramilarora, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption>\n</figure>"
  },
  "how-indias-automotive-clusters-rewired": {
    "picture_html": "<figure style=\"margin:1.6rem 0\">\n<img src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Ashok_Leyland_Factory.JPG/960px-Ashok_Leyland_Factory.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" alt=\"Ashok Leyland factory in Chennai, one of the anchor OEMs in India's deepest automotive cluster\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;display:block\"/>\n<figcaption class=\"gb-note\" style=\"margin-top:.4rem\">Ashok Leyland's Chennai factory anchors the Chennai/Sriperumbudur cluster this piece calls India's &quot;Detroit&quot; — the deepest, most export-oriented base in the just-in-time supplier geography it maps. <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashok_Leyland_Factory.JPG\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ashok Leyland Factory</a>, Nikhilb239, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption>\n</figure>"
  },
  "hsn-wise-historical-importexport-trends": {
    "picture_html": "<figure style=\"margin:1.6rem 0\">\n<img src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Fields_TR_Bazar_NH-181_Nilgiris_Aug25_A7CR_07388-HDR.jpg/960px-Fields_TR_Bazar_NH-181_Nilgiris_Aug25_A7CR_07388-HDR.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" alt=\"Bags of fertiliser in a newly planted Indian field, the import chapter this piece finds growing fastest\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;display:block\"/>\n<figcaption class=\"gb-note\" style=\"margin-top:.4rem\">Fertilisers (HS31) posted the highest eight-year growth rate among India's dozen largest imports, +118.9% since FY2018-19 — the bags pictured here, on a field in the Nilgiris, are the end point of that import chain. <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fields_TR_Bazar_NH-181_Nilgiris_Aug25_A7CR_07388-HDR.jpg\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Fields, TR Bazar, NH-181, Nilgiris</a>, Timothy A. Gonsalves, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption>\n</figure>"
  },
  "iem-conversion-historical-performance": {
    "picture_html": "<figure style=\"margin:1.6rem 0\">\n<img src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Bagalkot_cement_factory.jpg/960px-Bagalkot_cement_factory.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" alt=\"An Indian cement factory, the kind of manufacturing capacity an implemented IEM ultimately builds\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;display:block\"/>\n<figcaption class=\"gb-note\" style=\"margin-top:.4rem\">An Industrial Entrepreneurs Memorandum's Part B is exactly this — a filed investment intent that actually became a built factory, like this cement plant in Bagalkot, Karnataka. <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagalkot_cement_factory.jpg\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bagalkot cement factory</a>, Ravikiran KD, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption>\n</figure>"
  },
  "if-india-paid-brics-in-rupees-scenario": {
    "picture_html": "<figure style=\"margin:1.6rem 0\">\n<img src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/%E2%82%B92000_Indian_Rupee_Banknote.jpg/960px-%E2%82%B92000_Indian_Rupee_Banknote.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" alt=\"An Indian rupee banknote, the currency this piece models settling BRICS trade in instead of the US dollar\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;display:block\"/>\n<figcaption class=\"gb-note\" style=\"margin-top:.4rem\">The scenario this piece models turns on whether trade like India's $321.8 billion in BRICS imports could actually be settled in rupees, like the note pictured here, instead of dollars. <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E2%82%B92000_Indian_Rupee_Banknote.jpg\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">₹2000 Indian Rupee Banknote</a>, Ravi Dwivedi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption>\n</figure>"
  },
  "import-dependent-sectors-vs-government": {
    "picture_html": "<figure style=\"margin:1.6rem 0\">\n<img src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Udyog_Bhawan_1.jpg/960px-Udyog_Bhawan_1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" alt=\"Udyog Bhawan in New Delhi, home to the Ministries of Commerce and Industry that issue the schemes this piece tracks\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;display:block\"/>\n<figcaption class=\"gb-note\" style=\"margin-top:.4rem\">Udyog Bhawan in New Delhi houses the Ministries of Commerce &amp; Industry whose PIB-reported scheme announcements this piece checks against the same import chapters tracked in this blog's HSN bulletin. <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Udyog_Bhawan_1.jpg\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Udyog Bhawan</a>, Pinakpani, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption>\n</figure>"
  }
}